Hospital-acquired infection is an important measure of hospital safety and a major concern for international patients seeking cross-border care.
Our partner JCI-accredited hospitals have an infection rate of only 0.5%, well below the 1.2% average in European and US hospitals. Four key control pillars support this low rate:
1. Standardized aseptic practice: surgery and invasive examinations strictly follow sterile procedures, and staff adhere to hand hygiene standards;
2. Real-time infection monitoring: dedicated infection control teams routinely monitor wards, operating rooms and ICU areas, intervening promptly when abnormalities arise;
3. Isolation and protection: risk-based isolation for high-risk patients to prevent cross-infection;
4. Regular training and assessment: all medical staff receive infection control training and assessments, applying safety standards at every stage of care.
Whether for routine hospitalization, surgery or intensive care, the infection prevention system follows international standards to minimize postoperative and inpatient infection risks.
A comprehensive infection control system is an important safeguard for overseas patients receiving inpatient treatment.

