Many overseas patients wonder: why are medical costs in China far below Europe, the USA, Singapore and Japan, and does this mean lower quality?
Price differences reflect local cost structures rather than reduced quality of care, and arise from 5 key factors:
1. Labor cost differences: China's large pool of qualified medical staff and experienced specialists with extensive case experience keep labor costs lower than overseas;
2. Local medications and supplies: extensive use of compliant domestic drugs and medical supplies costs far less than imported products with comparable efficacy;
3. Operating cost advantage: hospital premises, logistics and maintenance cost less overall than in developed countries;
4. Economies of scale: large patient volumes and highly standardized surgical and treatment processes lower the cost per treatment;
5. No large commercial insurance markup: medical prices in many countries include substantial insurance intermediary markups, which are not added domestically.
Our partner JCI-accredited hospitals maintain international standards in diagnosis, treatment, medication and surgery; the overall cost structure is simply different.
International patients can receive comparable medical care with a more affordable budget.
Lower prices do not mean lower quality: the gap comes from cost structures, while medical safety and outcomes still strictly follow international standards.
